            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "v11-209-influence-douglas-adams",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: Douglas Adams",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Douglas Adams voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Douglas Adams' absurdist sci-fi humor. Satirical, witty, delightfully nonsensical. }}\n{{// @color #E67E22 }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Douglas Adams}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Douglas Adams\nUse cosmic bathos: vast absurdity meeting petty logistics. Let elegant setup collapse into bureaucratic nonsense, statistical improbability, or a technicality no one respected. Keep prose nimble, dry, and precise, with digressions that become the point.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Douglas Adams\nGive speakers different relationships to absurdity. One insists on procedure, one panics politely, one explains nonsense with confidence, one treats disaster as a scheduling issue. Dialogue should be rational in grammar and unreasonable in premise.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Douglas Adams\nUse baffled politeness, stalled objections, and sentences that lose faith in themselves halfway through. Repetition works when common sense hits an impossible wall. Tangents can swallow the original point, then return with a worse problem.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Douglas Adams\nMake physical beats inconveniently ordinary. Towels, forms, doors, cups of tea, buttons, panels, and badly designed machines should interrupt grand events. The body should remain comically practical inside cosmic scale.\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}Douglas Adams{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": false,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "v11-210-influence-terry-pratchett",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: Terry Pratchett",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip [PLAIN · multi-select influence] Terry Pratchett voice bias. Stacking more than two influences muddies all of them. Pratchett's satirical fantasy. Clever footnotes, wry observation, humanity found in absurdity. Discworld energy. }}\n{{// @color #F1C40F }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Terry Pratchett}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Style Guidelines — Terry Pratchett\nUse comic fantasy with moral steel. Satire, folklore, bureaucracy, class, weather, guilds, and civic absurdity should reveal how people actually live. Keep jokes precise and character-bound. Let comedy sharpen compassion while consequence stays intact.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Distinct Voice — Terry Pratchett\nGive speakers institutional texture: witches, guards, clerks, nobles, merchants, children, and monsters all carry jobs, habits, and social rank into speech. Wisdom often arrives in plain words. Foolishness often arrives in official language.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Imperfect Speech — Terry Pratchett\nUse comic correction, practical interruption, deadpan afterthought, and sudden sincerity. A line may start as a joke and land as ethics. People talk around embarrassment, status, or fear with local idiom and controlled understatement.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Body in Speech — Terry Pratchett\nUse work as gesture. Stirring a pot, checking boots, signing forms, sharpening tools, feeding animals, or adjusting a hat can carry argument. Physical comedy should reveal intelligence, class, fatigue, or care.\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}Terry Pratchett{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "identifier": "header_literary_fiction",
            "name": "<Literary Fiction Authors>",
            "system_prompt": false,
