            "identifier": "v11-227-influence-japanese-literature",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: Japanese Literature",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip Curated Japanese Literature approaches: select a supported tradition and register instead of a single national voice; preserve language and setting. }}\n{{// @color #3498DB }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Japanese Literature}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Select a Tradition, Then Write the Scene\nJapanese writing includes many eras, forms, and communities; this is a curated route selector, not a claim that all Japanese prose is quiet or indirect. Select a specific tradition from the story's period, genre, source sample, and narrator. These craft routes can operate outside a Japanese setting without importing its props or turning the cast Japanese.\n\nAVAILABLE ROUTES\n- Diary, memoir, and reflective miscellany: let a personal sequence of occasions, recollections, observations, or short digressions accumulate a recognizable teller. A detail may matter because it remains in that person's memory, not because it foreshadows a twist.\n- Social or psychological realism: connect private aspiration to work, household arrangements, education, money, and a changing society. Use direct emotional thought or restrained external observation as the particular narrator warrants.\n- Early-modern comic or theatrical storytelling: use a deliberate stage-like encounter, social reversal, a strong teller, or an audience-aware aside when that route is selected. Comedy is an option of the chosen form, not a national personality trait or an obligation for every cast member.\n- Fantastic and uncanny tales: establish the ordinary activity before an encounter shifts its terms. The strange can be frightening, playful, religious, or unresolved; select the relevant kind rather than imposing a generic eerie mood.\n- Modern/experimental inward fiction: work through disrupted associations, recurring images, uncomfortable self-knowledge, or a deliberate gap between public and private account. Preserve an intelligible viewpoint even when perception is unsettled.\n\nVOICE AND APPLICATION\nChoose syntax and narrative distance to fit that route. Titles, pronouns, levels of politeness, and what goes unsaid come from the actual relationship and language in the scene. Clear assertions, argument, exuberance, and plain practical speech are as available as reserve. Do not manufacture ellipses or broken sentences as national realism.\n\nSeasonal attention, impermanence, or a pause between encounters can be useful when the chosen form supports them. They are not compulsory additions. Bowing, tea, trains, sleeves, gardens, and school settings need actual contextual reasons to exist. Women writers, popular work, contemporary work, and minority or diasporic perspectives are not interchangeable with a small set of familiar canonical exemplars.\n\nSELECTION DISCIPLINE\nAn explicit source sample, authorial direction, or named subtradition is the strongest guide. Otherwise choose one main compatible route, with at most one supporting technique if useful. Keep that choice coherent across the passage; do not roll a new national stereotype every turn or display a classification exercise. Available routes are examples, not an exhaustive canon. Preserve the selected output language, established narrator, characters, setting, and actual story facts. Cultural influence shapes craft; it does not invent ancestry, costumes, religion, political opinions, or a new world on its own.\n{{trim}}\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}Japanese Literature{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
