            "identifier": "v11-228-influence-chinese-literature",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: Chinese Literature",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip Curated Chinese Literature approaches: select a supported tradition and register instead of a single national voice; preserve language and setting. }}\n{{// @color #3498DB }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Chinese Literature}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Select a Tradition, Then Write the Scene\nUse a specific Chinese-language literary tradition rather than a single timeless civilizational voice. Mainland, Taiwanese, Hong Kong, other Sinophone, and diasporic contexts have different histories; identify the relevant context when it matters. Classical and modern forms are options, not ingredients to mix indiscriminately.\n\nAVAILABLE ROUTES\n- Classical tale or episodic vernacular narrative: organize encounters around an intelligible journey, household, case, or chain of obligations. An oral storyteller's address, chapter cadence, or allusive comment belongs only to a selected telling mode.\n- Social and satirical realism: show livelihood, status, institutions, and family through particular transactions and consequences. An official formula can differ from the lived event; a character need not explain the entire social system aloud.\n- Intimate urban or domestic fiction: let attachment, money, desire, self-protection, and an uneven relationship shape the account. Small decisions can carry the scene without being lessons about universal duty.\n- Martial-arts or genre adventure: when selected, develop a legible code, competing loyalties, action geography, competence, and the cost of belonging to a group. Do not insert cultivation ranks, martial sects, or honor duels into another genre merely because the influence is Chinese.\n- Avant-garde, speculative, or formally experimental prose: permit rearranged chronology, unstable narrators, compressed estrangement, or imaginative systems when they serve this story. Keep the reader oriented about which person or narrative level makes a claim.\n\nVOICE AND APPLICATION\nA person's generation, region, education, work, intimacy, and situation determine their diction and directness. Not every elder speaks in proverbs, not every disagreement is face-saving, and not every character's motive reduces to filial obligation. Preserve differences among speakers without assigning them national speech tics.\n\nUse classical allusion only when the narrator or character plausibly knows it and it adds meaning; explain through context where needed. Render the selected output language naturally rather than simulating Chinese with broken syntax. Ritual, ancestry, tea, examinations, cities, factories, and migration are possible subjects, not a mandatory inventory.\n\nSELECTION DISCIPLINE\nAn explicit source sample, authorial direction, or named subtradition is the strongest guide. Otherwise choose one main compatible route, with at most one supporting technique if useful. Keep that choice coherent across the passage; do not roll a new national stereotype every turn or display a classification exercise. Available routes are examples, not an exhaustive canon. Preserve the selected output language, established narrator, characters, setting, and actual story facts. Cultural influence shapes craft; it does not invent ancestry, costumes, religion, political opinions, or a new world on its own.\n{{trim}}\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}Chinese Literature{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
