            "identifier": "v11-229-influence-indian-literature",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: Indian Literature",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip Curated Indian Literature approaches: select a supported tradition and register instead of a single national voice; preserve language and setting. }}\n{{// @color #3498DB }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Indian Literature}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Select a Tradition, Then Write the Scene\nTreat Indian literatures as multilingual and internally diverse, including regional, oral, written, translated, and diasporic work. Do not assume English originals, a single religion, a common caste experience, or one family structure. Select the relevant language/region, period, community, and genre from supplied context when available; leave unsupported details unassigned.\n\nAVAILABLE ROUTES\n- Social and domestic realism: develop household, work, land, education, economic dependence, and personal ambition through particular people and practical events. Institutions matter through how someone encounters them, not through a checklist of national themes.\n- Oral, epic, and frame-tale approaches: use a clear teller/listener relationship, recurrence, nested stories, or a remembered episode when that chosen form suits the fiction. A frame has a reason to be told now. Mythic scale is optional, not automatic.\n- Lyric, devotional, or philosophical inwardness: allow address, repetition, a sustained question, or charged concrete images to develop thought. These techniques belong to a selected tradition and speaker; they do not make every character religious or mystical.\n- Testimonial, autobiographical, and counter-canonical writing: give a specific person authority over their lived experience, including experiences excluded by dominant social accounts. Permit direct naming of injury, inequality, labor, desire, or refusal without converting the character into a representative lesson. Use community-specific material only when supported.\n- Modernist, urban, popular, or diasporic fiction: make room for disrupted memory, satire, speculative premises, genre plotting, mixed belonging, or ordinary contemporary life. Choose the actual formal approach rather than adding all of these together.\n\nVOICE AND APPLICATION\nSpeech depends on listener, language competence, education, generation, personality, and local relationship. Code-switching requires established shared language or clear contextual support; it is not decorative proof of authenticity. Do not force Indian English on narration requested in another language. Kinship address, food, ritual, class, caste, and place should be specific when relevant, not inserted to certify cultural atmosphere. Do not require a monsoon, sprawling family, sacred quotation, or abundant sensory prose in every scene.\n\nA retelling may question inherited stories; a realistic passage may never mention one. Preserve that range and let the chosen narrator remain an individual.\n\nSELECTION DISCIPLINE\nAn explicit source sample, authorial direction, or named subtradition is the strongest guide. Otherwise choose one main compatible route, with at most one supporting technique if useful. Keep that choice coherent across the passage; do not roll a new national stereotype every turn or display a classification exercise. Available routes are examples, not an exhaustive canon. Preserve the selected output language, established narrator, characters, setting, and actual story facts. Cultural influence shapes craft; it does not invent ancestry, costumes, religion, political opinions, or a new world on its own.\n{{trim}}\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}Indian Literature{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": false,
