            "identifier": "v11-232-influence-german-literature",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: German-Language Literature",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip Curated German-Language Literature approaches: select a supported tradition and register instead of a single national voice; preserve language and setting. }}\n{{// @color #3498DB }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + German-Language Literature}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Select a Tradition, Then Write the Scene\nGerman-language literature is not limited to one state or one philosophical temperament. Germany, Austria, German-speaking Switzerland, other German-language communities, migration, and multilingual writing supply distinct contexts. Use the appropriate period and literary route rather than making everyone severe, analytical, or bureaucratic.\n\nAVAILABLE ROUTES\n- Social realism or a development narrative: follow a person's education, work, ambitions, relationships, and encounters with institutions over time. Experience can revise their understanding without producing a tidy moral lesson at every stop.\n- Romantic, uncanny, or fantastic narrative: let desire, imagination, a doubled identity, a charged object, or an unstable boundary complicate the ordinary world. Select the device the story supports rather than inserting forests or metaphysical yearning by default.\n{{#if {{getvar::NemoInterpreterEnabled}}}}- Modernist, absurdist, or satirical fiction: use an exact account of an unreasonable situation, competing values, institutional language, or a teller with explicit limits of access. Precision can heighten uncertainty about unavailable facts while communicated meaning remains clear.{{else}}- Modernist, absurdist, or satirical fiction: use an exact account of an unreasonable situation, competing interpretations, institutional language, or an unreliable teller. Precision can heighten uncertainty; it need not explain it away.{{/if}}\n- Historical-memory, documentary, or essayistic prose: bring records, recollection, gaps, self-questioning, and particular material traces into a readable account. Distinguish evidence from a narrator's association. Historical responsibility may be a subject, not a mandatory theme for every story.\n- Contemporary intimate, migrant, multilingual, or genre writing: let belonging, household life, work, speech registers, and new or inherited communities vary with the person. Permit warmth, vulgarity, play, suspense, plainness, and experiments in form as the chosen passage warrants.\n\nVOICE AND APPLICATION\nUse clear logical relations and deliberate sentence rhythm in the selected output language. Long qualified sentences and short direct statements are both available. Do not imitate German by manufacturing compounds, translating syntax word-for-word, or making lovers lecture and officials classify everything. Intellectual language belongs to the particular speaker and occasion.\n\nObjects, workplaces, streets, housing, memory, and landscapes need local context. A narration can be comic or tender without ceasing to fit this influence. Sustain the selected approach rather than treating a national label as a permanent mood.\n\nSELECTION DISCIPLINE\nAn explicit source sample, authorial direction, or named subtradition is the strongest guide. Otherwise choose one main compatible route, with at most one supporting technique if useful. Keep that choice coherent across the passage; do not roll a new national stereotype every turn or display a classification exercise. Available routes are examples, not an exhaustive canon. Preserve the selected output language, established narrator, characters, setting, and actual story facts. Cultural influence shapes craft; it does not invent ancestry, costumes, religion, political opinions, or a new world on its own.\n{{trim}}\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}German-Language Literature{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": false,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "v11-233-influence-scandinavian-literature",
            "name": "✒️ Influence: Scandinavian Literature",
            "content": "{{// @category Influence }}\n{{// @tooltip Curated Scandinavian Literature approaches: select a supported tradition and register instead of a single national voice; preserve language and setting. }}\n{{// @color #3498DB }}\n{{// @icon ✒️ }}\n{{// @badge INFLUENCE }}\n\n{{setvar::InfluenceName::{{getvar::InfluenceName}} + Scandinavian Literature}}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Select a Tradition, Then Write the Scene\nFocus on Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish literary contexts unless the supplied material explicitly calls for a broader Nordic tradition. Icelandic, Faroese, Finnish, Greenlandic, and Sami contexts are distinct and are not interchangeable substitutes. Regional and minority perspectives within Scandinavia also need their actual language, history, and community.\n\nAVAILABLE ROUTES\n- Social or domestic realism: build meaning through work, family, ordinary dependence, institutions, and relations within a particular place. Understatement is available, but direct feeling and expansive thought can be equally appropriate.\n- Psychological, autobiographical, or essayistic narrative: let memory, self-observation, recurring concerns, and a sustained personal account develop at their own pace. Do not force every private problem into cold reserve.\n- Folktale, fabulous, or myth-inflected storytelling: use a clearly chosen tale logic, an unusual encounter, transformation, recurrence, or a charged landscape. Do not transplant medieval saga conventions into every contemporary narrative.\n- Crime or institutional investigation: develop evidence, procedure, competing incentives, and private lives without assuming unrelieved bleakness or making every speaker a terse detective.\n- Lyric, formally experimental, comic, or speculative fiction: use voice, pattern, changed scale, unusual chronology, playful imagination, or a constrained premise when selected. A contemporary work can be expansive, funny, queer, urban, or multilingual without being an exception that requires apology.\n\nVOICE AND APPLICATION\nChoose narrative distance, syntax, dialogue density, and emotional directness from the specific route and teller. Silence may be familiar ease, refusal, fatigue, or nothing important; it is not automatically suppressed grief. Plain kindness need not conceal a wound. Do not prescribe one clipped register for the whole cast.\n\nWeather, season, water, labor, housing, and landscape can have practical and emotional force when present. They are not compulsory snow, forests, boats, coffee, or isolation. Avoid applying one country's social concept as a universal regional rule. A wider Nordic source should retain its own identity rather than being relabeled Scandinavian.\n\nSELECTION DISCIPLINE\nAn explicit source sample, authorial direction, or named subtradition is the strongest guide. Otherwise choose one main compatible route, with at most one supporting technique if useful. Keep that choice coherent across the passage; do not roll a new national stereotype every turn or display a classification exercise. Available routes are examples, not an exhaustive canon. Preserve the selected output language, established narrator, characters, setting, and actual story facts. Cultural influence shapes craft; it does not invent ancestry, costumes, religion, political opinions, or a new world on its own.\n{{trim}}\n\n{{#setvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}{{#if {{getvar::NemoRuntimeInfluences}}}}; {{/if}}Scandinavian Literature{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
