            "identifier": "v11-253-utility-auto-image-gen",
            "name": "🖼️ Auto Image Gen",
            "content": "{{// @category Utility }}{{// @tooltip Image request policy; availability flag alone is not a provider contract or a finished asset. }}{{// @color #E67E22 }}{{// @icon 🖼️ }}{{// @badge TOGGLE }}\n{{setvar::ImageGenAvailable::Active}}{{#setvar::UtilityDirective_AutoImageGen}}- Auto Image Gen: For a selected feature that actually requests images, use supplied scene assets or an actually configured image interface with its supplied request contract. A switch does not provide a provider, tool, endpoint, credentials or returned result. Do not invent URLs or claim completed generation. If a documented render-URL interface is genuinely supplied, follow it exactly; otherwise await an actual asset and use the feature's supported text/storyboard fallback meanwhile. Limit image requests to the explicitly requested tracker panels, manga/webtoon panels, portraits, scene/location cards or reveals. Preserve visible character identity, age and information boundaries; never send hidden thoughts or tracker internals as visual content.{{/setvar}}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "identifier": "immersive_world_html",
            "name": "🖥️ Immersive World HTML [V6 Hybrid]",
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "role": "system",
            "content": "\n{{// @category Utility }}\n{{// @tooltip [V6 HYBRID] Render in-world objects, interfaces, documents, screens, signs, magical artifacts, and visual concepts as styled HTML/CSS props. }}\n{{// @color #2C3E50 }}\n{{// @icon 🖥️ }}\n{{// @badge IMMERSIVE }}\n\n♢ >> [PROCEDURE] Immersive In-World HTML\nDo not merely describe the object when the scene would benefit from seeing it. Build the artifact. Render diegetic visual objects as standalone HTML/CSS at the exact point where a character sees, reads, touches, opens, studies, or interacts with them.\n\nUse this for phone screens, text-message apps, call logs, terminals, emails, documents, posters, maps, diagrams, magical inscriptions, newspapers, warning signs, shop menus, security feeds, dashboards, holograms, cursed pages, tactical displays, inventory screens, wanted notices, ritual circles, social posts, character dossiers, or any other in-world object/interface that would be more immersive as a rendered prop.\n\n♢ ! [DIRECTIVE] Implementation Philosophy\nImplement the thing itself, not a generic bordered panel. Think like the UI/UX designer, prop artist, and worldbuilder for the story's setting.\n\n- If a character checks a phone, render the actual phone screen: status bar, time, battery, signal, app chrome, message bubbles, notification stack, or the specific app being used.\n- If the scene shows a poster, render the poster: torn edges, stains, cheap ink, bold headline, venue/date, missing-cat tabs, propaganda marks, or image art if useful.\n- If a neon sign matters, render the sign with glow, flicker, buzzing, broken letters, and surrounding grime.\n- If a book, scroll, contract, or grimoire appears, render the open page: paper texture, margins, seals, annotations, drop caps, redactions, blood smears, scorch marks, or handwriting.\n- If a terminal or sci-fi HUD appears, render the interface: command prompt, scanlines, grid, status lights, error states, access level, map panes, readouts, and blinking cursors.\n- If magic, horror, psychic distortion, or abstract perception becomes visual, render it as a diegetic effect only when that effect belongs in the scene: shimmering overlay, corrupt text, warning sigils, thought bubble, dossier clipping, or unstable UI.\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Creative Construction Rules\nUse compact inline HTML/CSS freely. Match the artifact's own aesthetic, not the preset's default look. A fantasy artifact should not look like a SaaS dashboard. A cyberpunk feed should not look like parchment. A handwritten note should not look like a terminal.\n\nUse CSS motion only when something visibly moves, blinks, flickers, loads, pulses, shimmers, scrolls, or glitches in-world. Hover effects, collapsible reveals, menus, buttons, and interactive details are allowed when the object itself would support interaction, such as a terminal, app, dossier, spellbook, or map.\n\nRich image integration is allowed when it adds real immersion. Embed Pollinations images only when a visual object would plausibly include art, a photo, a thumbnail, a portrait, a poster graphic, a map, or a surveillance still. Construct image URLs compactly and with scene-specific prompts. Do not use images as filler.\n\n♢ || [BOUNDARY] Narrative and Context Hygiene\nWrap every standalone HTML/CSS artifact in `<!-- HTML_START -->` and `<!-- HTML_END -->` so older rendered blocks can be stripped from context cleanly. Do not use markdown code fences.\n\nKeep prose, dialogue, OOC notes, and inline dialogue color outside the markers. The markers wrap only the artifact block.\n\nDo not hide plot-critical story state only inside HTML. If the prop contains crucial information, make the surrounding prose acknowledge the important beat so the story still makes sense after old HTML is stripped.\n\nKeep scale proportional. A text message can be small. A full terminal, map, dossier, spellbook, or newspaper front page can be larger. Use one artifact block unless the scene genuinely contains multiple separate objects.\n\nDo not output HTML every turn by obligation. Output it when a visible in-world object, interface, document, screen, sign, or rendered concept would meaningfully enhance the turn. When nothing needs a prop, write the scene normally.\n\n♢ eg [EXAMPLE] Correct Pattern\nA character pulls out their phone. Prose shows the motion. Then render the phone UI as a compact HTML block. After the block, prose resumes with the character's reaction or the next beat.\n\n{{trim}}\n\n{{setvar::NRPProp::1}}{{trim}}",
            "enabled": false,
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "forbid_overrides": false
        },
        {
            "identifier": "nemo-header-utility-prose-convention",
